WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.920
A growth cone is the spread fan at the tip of a growing axon.

2
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.420
Add brain membranes, and within minutes the fan folds into a thin tip.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.200
The protein responsible was purified from chick brain and named collapsin.

4
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.480
Today it is called SEMA 3A, and it works at about 10 picomolar.

5
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.320
Retinal growth cones did not respond.

6
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.160
What does the growth cone use to hear it?

7
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.670
Sema 3A is a secreted semaphorin.

8
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.810
Two research groups independently found its receptor.

9
00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.310
It is called neuropilin, and it binds Sema 3A tightly.

10
00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.150
But look inside the cell.

11
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:45.630
Its tail is only about 40 amino acids long, with nothing that looks like an enzyme.

12
00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:49.350
A receptor that catches a signal must also pass it on.

13
00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:50.830
Where is the wire?

14
00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:53.813
The answer was a second protein, plexin.

15
00:00:54.253 --> 00:00:58.833
In engineered cells, neither protein alone let Sema3A shrink the cell.

16
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:00.353
Together, they did.

17
00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:04.573
And the pair holds Sema3A more tightly than neuropilin alone.

18
00:01:05.353 --> 00:01:06.753
Plexins are a large family.

19
00:01:07.273 --> 00:01:09.653
Membrane semaphorins bind plexins directly.

20
00:01:10.253 --> 00:01:13.693
Most class III semaphorins reach plexins through neuropilin.

21
00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:15.737
Other work split the jobs.

22
00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:18.977
A neuropilin without its tail still supported collapse.

23
00:01:19.597 --> 00:01:24.417
A plexin stripped of its inner part blocked Sema3A responses in sensory neurons.

24
00:01:24.857 --> 00:01:26.157
So neuropilin catches.

25
00:01:26.777 --> 00:01:27.777
Plexin signals.

26
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:32.577
Inside the cell, plexin has a domain that acts as a GAP.

27
00:01:33.597 --> 00:01:40.877
AGAP is a protein that speeds GTP hydrolysis, and so switches a small GT -pace off.

28
00:01:41.537 --> 00:01:47.657
In the first case studied, plexin B1, the reported target is R -RAS, later work

29
00:01:47.657 --> 00:01:49.157
points to its relative Rap.

30
00:01:49.717 --> 00:01:52.337
Both help hold integrin adhesions to the substrate.

31
00:01:52.957 --> 00:01:54.677
Switch it off, and adhesion falls.

32
00:01:55.567 --> 00:02:01.787
Plexin signalling also engages Rho family G -T paces and proteins that act on actin

33
00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:06.367
and microtubules. The net result is a local loss of actin and adhesion.

34
00:02:07.027 --> 00:02:09.527
On one side of a growth cone, that means a turn.

35
00:02:09.987 --> 00:02:12.067
Everywhere at once, it means collapse.

36
00:02:12.787 --> 00:02:15.907
Keep the split. Neuropilin catches the semaphorin.

37
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:17.387
Plexin sends the signal.
